                        content: "The statue outside the station is the reason half the crowd is there, and the popular version of the story is roughly right but wrong in the details.\n\n**Hachiko was born in November 1923** near Odate, Akita, and came to Tokyo as a puppy to **Professor Hidesaburo Ueno**, who held the agricultural engineering chair at Tokyo Imperial University and effectively founded the discipline in Japan.\n\n**Ueno collapsed and died suddenly at the university on 21 May 1925**, about sixteen months later. Hachiko kept returning to Shibuya Station. He was cared for afterwards by a gardener who had worked at the Ueno house, and became nationally famous after an **Asahi Shimbun article in October 1932**.\n\nThe famous \"nine years and nine months\" is arithmetic from the two dates rather than a sourced figure — **Shibuya's own library says seven years, and the 1932 Asahi headline says seven** too. Both versions have a claim.\n\n**He died on 8 March 1935**, found near Inari-bashi over the Shibuya River, roughly where the Shibuya Stream car-park entrance is now. Sources give his age as 11 or 13 depending on whether they count in the Japanese style.\n\n**The cause of death was corrected in 2011.** University of Tokyo researchers re-examined his organs, preserved in formalin for 76 years, using MRI and histology. Filariasis had been recorded in 1935 as the sole cause; the 2011 work found an extensive **carcinosarcoma originating in the lung with metastasis to the heart**, alongside the parasites. The tumour was, in their words, also important as a cause of death.\n\n**The statue you see is not the original.** Andō Teru's 1934 statue — unveiled 21 April 1934, with Hachiko himself present at a ceremony of about 300 people — was surrendered under wartime metal requisition in 1944 and melted down in August 1945. The current one, by **Andō Takeshi, the original sculptor's son**, was unveiled on **15 August 1948**.\n\nOne correction worth making, because it circulates widely: **the statue did not move in 2023.** Its last relocation was **May 1989**, when it shifted about eight metres west and was turned from north-facing to east-facing. What did change is the **JR Hachiko ticket gate, relocated in January 2025**, so it no longer opens directly onto the square. The whole square is due to be rebuilt by **FY2034**, so a future move is plausible — but nothing has been announced.\n\nHachiko's taxidermied body is on display at the **National Museum of Nature and Science** in Ueno, Japan Gallery 2F. Admission ¥630, free under 18, 9:00–17:00, closed Mondays."
