                        content: "Fuji City has recorded whether Mount Fuji is visible at **08:00 every single day for over three decades**. Averaged across 1991–2023, the results demolish most travel advice:\n\n| Month | Visible |\n|---|---|\n| **December** | **84%** |\n| October – February | all above 65% |\n| May – August | all below 50% |\n| **July** | **23%** |\n\n**The climbing season, July to early September, is precisely when the mountain is least visible.** If your goal is to *see* Fuji rather than stand on it, come between **October and February** — and December is the single best month.\n\nAcross all of 2025 the whole mountain was visible at the 08:00 observation on **136 days, or 37.3% of the year**. That is the honest baseline: on any random day you have roughly a one-in-three chance.\n\n**Go early.** Fuji City's own multi-decade analysis found the **08:00 observation has the highest full-visibility rate of any hour** — cloud builds through the day. A first-train or first-bus departure from Shinjuku is not just about beating crowds; it materially changes whether you see anything.\n\nOne caveat: these are Fuji City figures, taken from the Shizuoka side. The pattern holds everywhere, but they are not Kawaguchiko-specific. Be sceptical of English blogs claiming \"near 100% visibility in December\" — they contradict the official record."
